{"module":"women","voice":"male","duration":156.1,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"The women who organized the movement","start":0.0,"end":2.24,"words":[[0.05,0.2],[0.2,0.58],[0.58,0.74],[0.74,1.35],[1.35,1.5],[1.5,2.04]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Black women did much of the organizing behind the movement's best-known moments, often without the microphone.","start":3.04,"end":9.82,"words":[[3.09,3.37],[3.37,3.71],[3.71,3.92],[3.92,4.2],[4.2,4.33],[4.33,4.47],[4.47,5.09],[5.09,5.58],[5.58,5.71],[5.71,6.27],[6.27,6.82],[6.82,7.44],[7.68,7.96],[7.96,8.37],[8.37,8.51],[8.51,9.27]]},{"el":"h0","text":"Founders","start":10.17,"end":11.06,"words":[[10.2,10.89]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Ida B. Wells investigated lynchings at the risk of her life.","start":11.76,"end":16.27,"words":[[11.82,12.11],[12.11,12.33],[12.69,12.98],[12.98,13.92],[13.92,14.5],[14.5,14.64],[14.64,14.79],[14.79,15.07],[15.07,15.22],[15.22,15.36],[15.36,15.72]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Mary Church Terrell co-founded the National Association of Colored Women (1896) and, at 89, won the lawsuit that desegregated Washington, D.C.'s restaurants (District of Columbia v. John R.","start":16.62,"end":30.27,"words":[[16.67,17.03],[17.03,17.39],[17.39,17.74],[17.74,18.46],[18.46,18.61],[18.61,19.11],[19.11,19.97],[19.97,20.11],[20.11,20.47],[20.47,20.83],[20.83,21.9],[22.15,22.44],[22.69,22.84],[22.84,23.48],[23.73,23.95],[23.95,24.09],[24.09,24.45],[24.45,24.66],[24.66,25.6],[25.6,26.24],[26.49,26.85],[26.85,27.5],[27.5,28.07],[28.07,28.21],[28.21,28.79],[28.79,29.0],[29.36,29.65],[29.65,29.79]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Thompson Co., 1953).","start":30.62,"end":33.01,"words":[[30.67,31.09],[31.09,31.33],[31.54,32.49]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Montgomery's women","start":33.91,"end":35.76,"words":[[33.97,35.05],[35.05,35.54]]},{"el":"s1","text":"In 1944 Rosa Parks investigated the rape of Recy Taylor for the NAACP, years before her bus protest.","start":36.46,"end":45.06,"words":[[36.51,36.67],[36.67,37.72],[37.72,38.13],[38.13,38.45],[38.45,39.5],[39.5,39.66],[39.66,39.98],[39.98,40.15],[40.15,40.55],[40.55,40.95],[40.95,41.12],[41.12,41.28],[41.28,42.41],[42.69,43.01],[43.01,43.34],[43.34,43.5],[43.5,43.74],[43.74,44.47]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Fifteen-year-old Claudette Colvin refused to give up her bus seat in March 1955, nine months before Parks.","start":45.41,"end":53.98,"words":[[45.43,46.65],[46.65,47.17],[47.17,47.69],[47.69,48.29],[48.29,48.47],[48.47,48.73],[48.73,48.9],[48.9,49.07],[49.07,49.33],[49.33,49.59],[49.59,49.77],[49.77,50.11],[50.11,51.59],[51.89,52.24],[52.24,52.67],[52.67,53.02],[53.02,53.45]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Colvin and Aurelia Browder were among the plaintiffs in Browder v. Gayle, the case that ended Montgomery's bus segregation.","start":54.33,"end":62.97,"words":[[54.38,54.85],[54.85,55.08],[55.08,55.62],[55.62,56.08],[56.08,56.24],[56.24,56.55],[56.55,56.7],[56.7,57.4],[57.4,57.55],[57.55,58.02],[58.02,58.25],[58.64,59.02],[59.29,59.45],[59.45,59.76],[59.76,59.99],[59.99,60.38],[60.38,61.23],[61.23,61.46],[61.46,62.39]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Jo Ann Robinson's Women's Political Council launched the boycott.","start":63.32,"end":67.03,"words":[[63.37,63.62],[63.62,63.74],[63.74,64.29],[64.29,64.66],[64.66,65.22],[65.22,65.65],[65.65,66.02],[66.02,66.14],[66.14,66.57]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Respectability: a strategy debated","start":67.93,"end":70.74,"words":[[67.98,69.0],[69.24,69.38],[69.38,69.99],[69.99,70.53]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Leaders chose Rosa Parks rather than Claudette Colvin as the face of the boycott, partly because Colvin was a pregnant teenager.","start":71.44,"end":80.23,"words":[[71.5,71.92],[71.92,72.34],[72.34,72.75],[72.75,73.09],[73.09,73.42],[73.42,73.67],[73.67,74.17],[74.17,74.67],[74.67,74.84],[74.84,75.0],[75.0,75.34],[75.34,75.5],[75.5,75.67],[75.67,76.25],[76.54,76.96],[76.96,77.38],[77.38,77.88],[77.88,78.13],[78.13,78.29],[78.29,78.96],[78.96,79.71]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Historians and activists still debate “respectability politics”: whether presenting the most sympathetic plaintiffs helped win public support, or excluded people and narrowed whose dignity counted.","start":80.58,"end":93.06,"words":[[80.62,81.36],[81.36,81.57],[81.57,82.23],[82.23,82.53],[82.53,82.97],[82.97,83.99],[83.99,84.65],[84.91,85.2],[85.2,85.86],[85.86,86.01],[86.01,86.37],[86.37,87.1],[87.1,87.76],[87.76,88.13],[88.13,88.35],[88.35,88.79],[88.79,89.23],[89.48,89.56],[89.56,90.22],[90.22,90.58],[90.58,90.8],[90.8,91.24],[91.24,91.46],[91.46,91.97],[91.97,92.56]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Organizers and strategists","start":93.96,"end":95.88,"words":[[94.01,94.65],[94.65,94.87],[94.87,95.72]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Ella Baker built the NAACP's branches, ran the SCLC's first office and guided SNCC.","start":96.58,"end":102.96,"words":[[96.62,96.84],[96.84,97.21],[97.21,97.5],[97.5,97.64],[97.64,98.6],[98.6,99.25],[99.51,99.73],[99.73,99.88],[99.88,100.54],[100.54,100.83],[100.83,101.12],[101.12,101.34],[101.34,101.78],[101.78,102.44]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Septima Clark's Citizenship Schools taught thousands of adults to read and pass voter tests.","start":103.31,"end":109.78,"words":[[103.35,103.95],[103.95,104.37],[104.37,105.22],[105.22,105.64],[105.64,105.9],[105.9,106.57],[106.57,106.74],[106.74,107.25],[107.25,107.42],[107.42,107.68],[107.68,107.93],[107.93,108.18],[108.18,108.61],[108.61,109.12]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Diane Nash led the Nashville sit-ins and kept the Freedom Rides going in 1961.","start":110.13,"end":115.82,"words":[[110.18,110.62],[110.62,110.84],[110.84,111.05],[111.05,111.2],[111.2,111.63],[111.63,112.07],[112.07,112.29],[112.29,112.58],[112.58,112.72],[112.72,113.16],[113.16,113.52],[113.52,113.89],[113.89,114.03],[114.03,115.27]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Fannie Lou Hamer was beaten in a Winona, Mississippi, jail in 1963 for organizing voters.","start":116.17,"end":123.54,"words":[[116.19,116.5],[116.5,116.65],[116.65,117.04],[117.04,117.27],[117.27,117.65],[117.65,117.8],[117.8,117.96],[117.96,118.57],[118.84,119.53],[119.8,120.18],[120.18,120.34],[120.34,121.57],[121.57,121.72],[121.72,122.41],[122.41,122.95]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Dorothy Height helped plan the March on Washington, where almost no women were allowed to speak.","start":123.89,"end":130.03,"words":[[123.94,124.41],[124.41,124.71],[124.71,125.1],[125.1,125.41],[125.41,125.56],[125.56,125.87],[125.87,126.03],[126.03,126.72],[126.99,127.22],[127.22,127.76],[127.76,128.0],[128.0,128.38],[128.38,128.54],[128.54,128.92],[128.92,129.08],[129.08,129.46]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Today","start":130.93,"end":131.62,"words":[[130.98,131.41]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Alicia Garza, Patrisse Cullors and Opal Tometi started Black Lives Matter in 2013.","start":132.32,"end":139.05,"words":[[132.36,132.79],[132.79,133.21],[133.51,134.02],[134.02,134.45],[134.45,134.7],[134.7,135.13],[135.13,135.64],[135.64,136.15],[136.15,136.49],[136.49,136.92],[136.92,137.26],[137.26,137.43],[137.43,138.45]]},{"el":"s4","text":"The #SayHerName campaign (2014) brought attention to Black women killed by police, such as Breonna Taylor in 2020.","start":139.4,"end":147.95,"words":[[139.43,139.58],[139.58,140.47],[140.47,141.0],[141.0,141.81],[142.07,142.37],[142.37,142.89],[142.89,143.04],[143.04,143.34],[143.34,143.71],[143.71,144.01],[144.01,144.23],[144.23,144.68],[144.94,145.24],[145.24,145.38],[145.38,145.9],[145.9,146.28],[146.28,146.43],[146.43,147.39]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Black women are among the most reliable voters in America, and their organizing has decided close elections.","start":148.3,"end":155.24,"words":[[148.34,148.62],[148.62,148.98],[148.98,149.05],[149.05,149.33],[149.33,149.48],[149.48,149.83],[149.83,150.47],[150.47,150.9],[150.9,151.04],[151.04,151.61],[151.86,152.07],[152.07,152.28],[152.28,152.92],[152.92,153.13],[153.13,153.7],[153.7,154.06],[154.06,154.7]]}]}